{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790923872,"nonce":"Kb8fu68DH3RooVStY3J7TbzxynI=","title":"The webhook signature cannot be rotated, cannot coexist with a bearer token, is untested and undocumented, and oto.notification.v1 has no written compatibility promise","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0055 §1 makes `oto.notification.v1` the plugin API and asks for two things a stranger needs to build on it: \"a **signature header** (HMAC over the body with a per-channel secret, so a receiver can tell oto sent it), and a written **compatibility promise** — additive changes only within `v1`; a removal or a change of meaning is `v2`, sent side by side for a stated period.\" ⚠️ The signature is not missing — it half-exists. A webhook Connection may carry a `webhook_signing_secret` credential (`db/migrations/00075_a_channel_answers_to_a_connection.sql:83-88`), and `Channel.signaturePayload` (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/channel.go:166-177`) sets `X-Oto-Signature: sha256=\u003chex HMAC-SHA256 of the body\u003e`. What it lacks is everything that makes a signature usable by someone who did not write it: there is one secret and no overlap, so rotating it breaks every receiver the instant it is saved; the signed base is the body alone, with no timestamp, so a captured request verifies forever; a Connection holds ONE credential (`channel_connections.credential_id`, ADR 0047 \"A webhook connection's shared credential is one row, one kind\"), so a receiver that needs a bearer token — incident.io's HTTP alert source does — cannot also be signed; no test anywhere exercises it (the only `CredSigningSecret` in a `_test.go` is Slack's own, `internal/channels/providers/slack/errors_test.go:149`); and no operator-facing page says the header exists. The compatibility promise lives only in a code comment (`internal/channels/render/webhookjson/envelope.go:12-14`, \"both are emitted for a release\") and in field comments citing §H.10; SPEC §H.10 itself (`docs/design/SPEC.md:5048`) states no promise, and there is no `docs/setup/` page for the webhook at all.\n\nThe secret is per-Connection, not per-channel. ADR 0055 §1 says \"per-channel\"; migration 00075 rules it \"a connection-level credential like the others, never a channel-level one\", and nothing in ADR 0055 argues for moving it. This ticket keeps it on the Connection and treats §1's wording as loose.\n\n**The demonstration.** Configure a Connection with a signing secret, rotate the credential in Settings → Connections (`web/src/features/settings/ChannelsSection.tsx:191-197` shows the rotation date): the next delivery is signed with the new secret only, and a receiver still holding the old one rejects it. Separately, `channel.go:118-127` sets `X-Oto-Delivery-Id` and `X-Oto-Content-Hash` and THEN applies the configured headers, with a comment claiming \"CheckHeaders already refused the reserved names\" — but `forbiddenHeaders` (`internal/channels/providers/webhook/headers.go:25-32`) names only Authorization, Proxy-Authorization, Content-Length, Host, Transfer-Encoding and Connection. A channel configured with its own `X-Oto-Delivery-Id` header overrides oto's idempotency handle. The signature header is safe only because it is set last (`channel.go:128-133`).\n\n**Why it matters.** Every integration ADR 0055 §§2–3 enables — a catalog mapping, an operator's bridge — is a stranger's receiver deciding whether to trust a request. A signature that cannot rotate gets turned off the first time a secret leaks; one that cannot coexist with a bearer token is unavailable for exactly the incident tools 0055 is for; and a promise that lives in a Go comment is not a promise anyone outside the repo can hold oto to. The header's base can still change once, now, because it has never been documented — after this ticket it cannot.\n\n**Where.** `internal/channels/providers/webhook/channel.go` (signing, header order), `provider.go:29-43,114` (credential kinds), `headers.go:25-32` (reserve every `X-Oto-*` name), `db/migrations/` (a second credential slot on `channel_connections`, or a signing secret that is not the `credential_id` row — the implementer picks one and says why; ADR 0047 deferred this \"for a case that does not exist yet\", and 0055 is that case), the Connection form in `web/src/features/settings/ChannelsSection.tsx`, and a new `docs/setup/webhook.md` (synced into the docs site by `docs-site/scripts/sync-docs.mjs`, `setup/` is autogenerated in the sidebar).\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** A Connection can carry a bearer/basic credential AND a signing secret at once; the signature covers a timestamp header as well as the body, in one documented base string; rotating the signing secret keeps the previous secret signing alongside the new one for a stated overlap (both signatures in the header, the receiver accepting either), after which the old one stops; every `X-Oto-*` header name is refused in a channel's configured headers; tests prove a body signed with the documented algorithm verifies, a tampered body or stale timestamp does not, both signatures appear during an overlap, and a configured `X-Oto-Delivery-Id` is refused at save; `docs/setup/webhook.md` exists and states the envelope's keys and their meanings including the five Incident facts and `incident.id` as the de-duplication key, how to verify the signature with a worked example, and the compatibility promise in ADR 0055 §1's words with the dual-emit period given as a number.\n\n**Blocked by.** None.\n\n**Mode.** AFK. Source: ADR 0055 §1, ticket-filing session 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}